#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
perry-goals — the deterministic read of a project's goals, and its writer.

DESIGN-005 steps 2 and 3. The third of Perry's read contracts, after
`perry-task/list/*` and `perry-decide/list/*`, and the last one needed before a
front-end can show a whole project without opening a single markdown file.

**It parses nothing itself.** Every field below comes from
`viewer/parsers.py § load_snapshot`, the same code `perry-state` and the viewer
use. That is not laziness — a second parser of one file is the defect this
project has now hit twice: `perry-task` placed board cells by resolved header
name while `viewer/parsers.py` read them by position, and on a board with one
extra column every task's owner was reported as its track with the linter
calling it clean. A fourth reader of `OKR.md` would be a fourth chance at the
same shape. This tool composes and reshapes; it does not read.

What it adds over `perry-state --json`, which already carries the nested
structures: a **flat `krs` array**. A front-end wants "every KR, with where it
sits and how far along it is" — walking objectives→krs and re-deriving progress
is exactly the estimating `schema/README.md` forbids, done in a repo Perry's
tests cannot reach.

## The write side (TASK-037 / TASK-042)

`commit` is the first write path, because `OKR.md § Commitments` is the one
section with a live consumer and no writer at all: `modes/pipeline.md` and
`modes/queue.md` both name it as their spine, and until now the procedure was
prose in `goals/reference/phases.md § commit <promise>` that an agent followed
by hand. That page is this tool's acceptance criteria, rule for rule.

**`OKR.md` is never re-rendered.** `Okr` below edits lines in place, and cells
in place inside those lines — see `splice_cell`. A writer that round-trips
prose through a parsed model produces a file that still passes `perry-lint` and
no longer reads the way its author wrote it, which DESIGN-005 § 5.5 rates worse
than having no writer at all.

**`okr.jsonl` is the truth for the register, and `OKR.md § Commitments` is the
projection of it** (ADR-007 decision 2, TASK-123). `commit` reads that store
BEFORE it decides anything, and a cell the two disagree about is a hand edit it
refuses to act on — `register_drift` and `check_register_drift`. It does not
re-render the file to get there; it compares, and once the comparison passes,
every cell read out of the markdown below is provably the store's own value.
`--accept-hand-edit` is the way past, with the meaning it already has here: the
FILE's value becomes the new truth and the next write records it.

**The log is the truth, the file is the view** — accepted by the user on
2026-08-17 (DESIGN-005 § 9, last entry) *before* this writer was built, exactly
so it would not be built against "the file is the truth". Every write appends
an event to `.perry/events.jsonl`, and a row the tool wrote whose cells no
longer match the log is a **hand edit**: reported and reconciled with the
user's value, never overwritten with the log's.

**This tool writes `OKR.md` and `phase/` and nothing else.** `BOARD.md`,
`journal/` and `decisions/` belong to other lanes, and `SKILL.md § The
hand-off contract` names all three as cases that must refuse. `assert_owned`
is that rule as code, and the board-side link — a `Commitment` cell carrying an
`Id` from this table — is printed as a hand-off for `/perry work` to write.

Usage:
  perry-goals list [--json] [--level overall|phase]

  perry-goals krs [--json] [--phase <NNN>]
      The current phase's key results, printed from `linkage.jsonl`.
      READ-ONLY, and the only surface for them: TASK-157 removed the KR table
      from `phase/<NNN>-<slug>.md` because those four facts were written there
      AND in the register, with nothing comparing the two and the markdown copy
      already stale. DESIGN-013 § 5.1 — a fact with a schema lives in exactly
      one store.

  perry-goals krs --level overall [--json] [--version <v>|all]
      The OKR's own key results, printed from `okr.jsonl`. READ-ONLY, and the
      only surface for them: TASK-236 removed the KR tables from `OKR.md` for
      the same reason TASK-157 removed the phase one, after `perry-okr diff`
      proved the store reproduced all 38 rows byte for byte. Defaults to the
      CURRENT version block; `--version all` prints every one, and
      `--version "v2: 2026-08-17"` prints that one. The `### Objective N`
      headings stay in `OKR.md` — they are what these rows hang from.

  perry-goals commit --actor <who> --track <t> --promise <text> --to <whom> --due <when>
                     [--by-when-note <prose>]
  perry-goals commit --actor <who> --id <Id> [--due <when>] [--by-when-note <prose>]
                                [--promise <t>] [--to <whom>]
                                [--discharged-by <text>]
  perry-goals commit --actor <who> --close <Id> [--discharged-by <text>]
  perry-goals commit --actor <who> --miss <Id> --reason <text>
  perry-goals commit --actor <who> --migrate       split a pre-TASK-091 `By when` column

  perry-goals link --actor <who> <TASK-ID> <KR-ID>
  perry-goals link --actor <who> --alias <PROJECT-ID> "<the other name>"
  perry-goals link --actor <who> --unlinked <TASK-ID>
  perry-goals link --actor <who> --project <PROJECT-ID> <KR-ID> "<name>"

  perry-goals measure <KR-ID> [--okr-version V] --check S --value N
                      --evidence <path> --actor <who>
  perry-goals check <KR-ID> [--okr-version V] --id S --direction D
                    --target N [--baseline N] --label "<words>" --actor <who>
      DESIGN-022 § 5.3. Each appends ONE `measurement` / `check` record to
      `linkage.jsonl` and one `measure` / `check` event. A phase KR is named
      by its id; an overall KR by its id and, when more than one OKR version
      carries that id, `--okr-version "v4: 2026-09-15"` — the full label,
      matched exactly. `--okr-version ""` names the phase KR. D is one of
      increase · decrease · at_least · at_most · done. Both take `--dry-run`,
      and both REQUIRE `--actor <who>` (USER-950): without it, exit 2.

  perry-goals kr add <KR-ID> [--okr-version V] --objective O --text "<words>"
                 [--set FIELD=VALUE…] --reason "<why>" --actor <who>
  perry-goals kr restate <KR-ID> [--okr-version V] --set FIELD=VALUE…
                 --reason "<why>" --actor <who>
  perry-goals kr withdraw <KR-ID> [--okr-version V] --reason "<why>" --actor <who>
      DESIGN-022 § 5.7. `add` appends a `kr` record; `restate` / `withdraw`
      append a `kr_revision` record — to `linkage.jsonl` for a phase KR, to
      `okr.jsonl` for an overall KR. No record is rewritten and `OKR.md` is
      not written. A withdrawn KR is terminal and its id is never reused.
      All three take `--dry-run` and REQUIRE `--actor` and `--reason`.

  perry-goals objective add <O-ID> --text "<title>" --reason "<why>" --actor <who>
      TASK-475. Appends one `objective` record — `phase`, `id`, `title` — to
      `linkage.jsonl` for the CURRENT phase, and one `objective_add` event.
      The phase's objectives are what `kr add --objective` resolves against,
      and `phase new` writes none, so this runs after `phase new` and before
      the phase's first `kr add`. File order is objective order. Refused on a
      scored phase, an id that is not `O<n>`, and an id the phase already has.
      Takes `--dry-run`; REQUIRES `--actor` and `--reason`.

  perry-goals phase new --slug <slug> --body-file <path> --actor <who>
  perry-goals phase activate --phase <NNN> --actor <who>
  perry-goals phase close [--phase <NNN>] --actor <who>
      The phase lifecycle (TASK-474); `phase` must be the first argument.
      `new` assigns the next unused number, writes phase/<NNN>-<slug>.md from
      the supplied body, stamps Started and Status, and activates it in one
      locked operation. `close` snapshots to phase/snapshots/, flips Status to
      scored in place, and clears phase/CURRENT. All three take --dry-run and
      --json and REQUIRE --actor. Refusals and gates:
      `goals/reference/phases.md`.

  perry-goals draft create|show|update|approve|abandon|finalize …
      A planning draft, plans/okr/<date>-<slug>.md (TASK-444, DESIGN-020 §
      5.5); `draft` must be the first argument. Flags per mode, the approval
      rule and the unsupported finalize: `goals/reference/planning.md`.

  Every writer requires a non-empty, single-line `--actor` (USER-950).

  `link` appends to `linkage.jsonl` for the CURRENT phase.
  <KR-ID> may also be a Project id or a registered alias, resolved in that
  order; anything that does not resolve to exactly ONE KR is REFUSED with its
  candidates named. Nothing here is fuzzy-matched, and nothing here writes a
  `target` or a `current` — see `goals/reference/linkage.md`.

  --due <when>    TYPED: an ISO date (2026-09-30) or an SLA token (3d, 2w).
                  Anything else is refused, in every language.
  --by-when-note <prose>
                  PROSE: how the deadline was worded to the party it was
                  promised to. **Never validated.** No regex asks it anything.

  --root <path>   project root; default $PERRY_PROJECT, else walk up from cwd
  --json          machine-readable result
  --dry-run       print the plan; write nothing
  --accept-hand-edit
                  proceed over a row the log and the file disagree about,
                  taking the FILE's value as the new truth

Exit codes:
  0  read, or written
  1  refused — the reason is printed, and nothing was written
  2  bad invocation

No LLM, no external dependencies (stdlib only). DESIGN-005.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import json
import math
import os
import sys
import re
from contextlib import contextmanager
from dataclasses import asdict, is_dataclass
from datetime import date, datetime
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
PERRY_HOME = Path(os.environ.get("PERRY_HOME") or HERE.parent).resolve()
SCHEMA_PATH = PERRY_HOME / "schema" / "state-schema.json"
TEMPLATE_PATH = PERRY_HOME / "goals" / "state" / "OKR_TEMPLATE.md"

sys.path.insert(0, str(PERRY_HOME / "viewer"))
sys.path.insert(0, str(PERRY_HOME / "bin"))
import parsers as P  # noqa: E402
